## Revision (2026-09-07) — defect fix: `/settings/notifications` hangs on the loading skeleton

**Trigger.** Running `documentation/40-engineering/ui-ux-guidelines.md`'s review checklist
against the RENDERED `/settings/notifications` screen (phase 4's own closing step, recorded
as manual) found the card stuck on `aria-busy="true"`/`aria-label="Carregando"` forever.
Root cause, confirmed by executing in the page: zero service-worker registrations, so
`navigator.serviceWorker.ready` never resolves — and it never rejects either, so there is no
error path. `NotificationsCard.tsx`'s `load()` does
`const subscribed = (await getCurrentDeviceEndpoint()) !== null;` before dispatching ANY
state, and `getCurrentDeviceEndpoint()` awaits that promise — so the reducer can never leave
`kind: "loading"`, no matter what `Notification.permission` reads. This defeats PRD AC-9's
`denied` ("bloqueadas", the way back) branch specifically, since that branch needs no
subscription lookup at all, and violates guidelines §8's "a request failure is shown inline
next to the action that failed, never a silent indefinite wait" for the general case.

**Scope discipline applied.** Per this project's "Browser-API work" split
(`docs/context/methodology.md`), only the decidable half is testable/fixable in
`src/shared`; `NotificationsCard.tsx`'s async orchestration is glue, verified on the device,
and is NOT itself under test here. What this session adds is the type-and-behavior contract
the fix must satisfy at the reducer boundary — the exact shape the glue's rewritten `load()`
can now legally construct without waiting on the hung/failed lookup.

