## Revision (2026-09-07) — `test-reviewer` R-TRIVIAL-ASSERT (blocking), second round

**Finding, accepted as correct.** The three "loaded omits subscribed" tests added in the prior
round proved only that `reduceNotificationsSettings` TOLERATES a `loaded` event without
`subscribed` — a tolerance the runtime already had before that round (all three passed
unchanged; the only new RED was a `tsc -b` type mismatch). They never asserted the actual
defect: `NotificationsCard.tsx:47-48` awaits `getCurrentDeviceEndpoint()` (which awaits
`navigator.serviceWorker.ready`, a promise that can hang forever and never rejects) BEFORE
dispatching any state. An implementer could satisfy every assertion those three tests made by
loosening the event's TYPE alone while leaving `load()`'s await ORDER untouched, and the hang
would remain. Type permits omission != component actually dispatches without waiting.

**Fix applied — new decidable seam.** Per `docs/context/methodology.md`'s "Browser-API work"
split (the same move already applied once this phase for `src/sw.ts`'s payload parsing, see
`test/push-payload-parse.test.ts`), `load()`'s two-step sequence is pulled out into a new pure,
DOM-free orchestrator, `src/shared/notifications-load.ts` exporting
`loadNotificationsSettings(permission, checkSubscribed, dispatch)`, where `checkSubscribed` is
an INJECTED `() => Promise<boolean>` port. `NotificationsCard.tsx` becomes the thin adapter
supplying the real port (wrapping `getCurrentDeviceEndpoint()`) and the real
`Notification.permission` value; the plan is being amended in parallel to add this module as a
CREATE task and to rewrite `load()`/the `permissions.query` `handleChange` listener to call it,
matching this contract exactly.

Contract pinned by `test/notifications-load.test.ts` (new):
1. Dispatches `{ type: "loaded", permission }` SYNCHRONOUSLY, before any `await` — proven with a
   subscription-check port that deliberately NEVER SETTLES (the exact shape of the real hang),
   so no type-only fix can satisfy the assertion.
2. For `denied`/`default` permissions, NEVER calls `checkSubscribed` at all — asserted via
   `expect(checkSubscribed).not.toHaveBeenCalled()`, and the dispatched event is replayed
   through the real `reduceNotificationsSettings` to confirm it lands on `blocked` /
   `explainer` respectively (effect check, not a was-called-only assertion).
3. For a `granted` permission, DOES call and await `checkSubscribed` when its result is
   genuinely needed for the enabled/subscribed distinction — proven with a manually-deferred
   promise: `dispatch` is asserted to have fired only once (the initial `loaded`) before the
   port resolves, and twice (adding `permission-changed` with `subscribed: true`) only after it
   is explicitly resolved and awaited. This guards against overcorrecting into never checking.
4. If `checkSubscribed` rejects for a `granted` permission, dispatches the EXISTING
   `toggle-failed` event (reusing the prior round's R-DUPLICATE-driven mapping — no new event
   type introduced) and the replayed reducer state lands on an ACTIONABLE `enabled` view, not
   `failed` and not stuck `loading`.

